Objective:

 Exploit Kerberos TGS ticket issuance to obtain offline-crackable hashes
 from service accounts with SPNs set, without any elevated privileges.

Environment:

 . Attacker: Kali Linux
 . Target: redteamlab.local DC
 . Target account: svc_backup (SPN: HTTP/iis.redteamlab.local)
 . Starting access: valid low-privilege domain credentials

Why It Works:

 The KDC will issue a TGS to any authenticated domain user for any SPN that exists —
 with no authorization check on whether the requester is actually allowed to use that service.
 The TGS is encrypted with the service account's own password-derived key.
 Because the key is password-derived (not random), an attacker who captures the TGS can attempt to crack it offline
 trying candidate passwords until one produces matching encryption, revealing the plaintext password.
 No network contact with the DC is needed after the initial TGS request, and no account lockout is possible
 during offline cracking.

Commands:

 bash 
 # Find Kerberoastable accounts and request TGS hashes
 impacket-GetUserSPNs redteamlab.local/jdoe:Password123! \
  -dc-ip 192.168.56.101 -request

 # Crack the captured hash
 john --format=krb5tgs --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt hash.txt
 # or
 hashcat -m 13100 hash.txt /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt

Findings:

 . svc_backup — SPN: HTTP/iis.redteamlab.local
 . TGS hash captured successfully
 . Password cracked: Password123Findings


What Makes a Good Kerberoasting Target:

Two independent factors — both must be considered:

 1.Privilege of the account — what can it access if cracked?
   (check BloodHound for group memberships, local admin rights, ACL paths)
 2. Crackability of the password — service accounts are often set once and never rotated,
    making them vulnerable to dictionary attacks

Real-World Scenario:

 Service accounts in real organizations are notorious for weak, never-rotated passwords
 set by a developer years ago and forgotten. Kerberoasting is low-noise
 (one legitimate TGS request per target account), requires only a valid domain user credential,
 and produces an offline-crackable artifact — making it one of the most commonly abused techniques in
 real engagements.

Detection:

   . Event ID 4769 (Kerberos Service Ticket Request) with encryption type 0x17 (RC4) — modern environments prefer AES, so RC4 TGS requests stand out
   . High volume of 4769 events in short time from one account (mass Kerberoasting)

Key Lessons;

    . Kerberos does not check authorization before issuing a TGS — it only checks authentication
    
    . RC4 vs AES encryption on TGS tickets is a detection signal — use AES-encrypted requests where possible for stealth
    
    . A cracked service account with no privileges is still a finding — document it, don't manufacture a path that doesn't exist

Interview Explanation:

"Kerberoasting exploits the fact that any authenticated domain user can request a Kerberos service ticket
 for any SPN-set account, and that ticket is encrypted with the service account's password-derived key.
 I used impacket-GetUserSPNs to request TGS hashes for svc_backup, then cracked the hash offline with hashcat,
 recovering the password Password123 without triggering any account lockout or requiring elevated privileges."

